#macro(readmeHeader)
#if($envMap.language.equals("Cpp"))
#if ($use_dpde)
C++ ${envMap.idlFileName} Dynamic Discovery Example
#else
C++ ${envMap.idlFileName} Static Discovery Example
#end
#else
#if ($use_dpde)
C ${envMap.idlFileName} Dynamic Discovery Example
#else
C ${envMap.idlFileName} Static Discovery Example
#end
#end
=====================

An example publication and subscription pair to send and receive the type
provide by user.

#if ($use_static_udp)
This example uses static UDP interface configuration.

#end
#if ($use_dpde)
Discovery of endpoints is done with the dynamic-endpoint discovery.
#else
Discovery of endpoints is done with the static-endpoint discovery.
#end

Purpose
=======

#if ($use_static_udp)
This example uses static UDP interface configuration. Using this API
the UDP transport is statically configured. This is useful in systems
which are not able to return the installed UDP interfaces (name, IP
address, mask...).

#end
#if (!$use_mag)
This example shows how to perform basic publish-subscribe communication.
#else
This example shows how to create an application using Micro Application
Generation and XML files.
#end
#if ($use_shared_memory)

This example only uses shared memory as transport. For this reason both the
publisher and subscriber applications need to run on the same OS.
#end

#if ($use_dpde)
This example performs endpoint discovery dynamically: state of remote endpoints
are propagated automatically by built-in discovery endpoints, and the user does
not need to manually configure remote endpoint state.
#else
This example performs endpoint discovery statically: state of remote remote
endpoints are manually configured by the user, and only configured endpoints
can be discovered.
#end

#if (${use_listener})
Subscriber application creates a DataReader which uses a listener to receive
notifications about new samples and matched publishers. These notifications are
received in the middleware thread (instead of the application thread).
#else
Subscriber application creates a DataReader which uses a Waitset to receive
notifications about new samples and matched publishers. These notifications are
received in the application thread (instead of the middleware thread).
#end

#if ($use_crc)
This example configures the CRC QoS to allow the generation of a checksum value.
It determines which checksums are allowed and whether they should be sent.
#end

#if ($use_zcv2)
In addition, the example enables the Zero Copy v2 interface to communicate
with shared memory.
#end

#if ($use_content_filter)
In addition, this example demonstrates how to configure a content filter for a
DataReader.
#end

#end ##  #macro(readmeHeader)


#macro(readmeHowToCompileAndRun)
How to Compile and Run
======================

--------------------
Compiling with CMake
--------------------
Before compiling, set environment variable RTIMEHOME to the Connext Micro
installation directory.

#if ($use_secure)
Set environment variable OPENSSLHOME to the folder where OpenSSL libraries
and header files are found. These libraries should match those used to build
RTI Connext Micro.

If you built the RTI Connext Micro libraries using an external libxml2
version than the one shipped with the product, environment variable SYSLIBXML2
must be set properly to link the external library, and LIBXML2HOME can be used
to inform the build system on where to find it (if not located in a system path).

#end

#if ($use_cert_api_only)
In order to compile the CERT example it is necessary to first compile the
RTI Connext Micro libraries using the CERT profile. To compile RTI Connext Micro
with the CERT profile, use one of the CERT architectures as the target to
rtime-make. To see a list of architectures with the CERT profile, use:

rtime-make --list

Architectures ending with CERT, such as x64Linux6gcc13.3.0CERT, compiles
RTI Connext Micro with CERT profile:

rtime-make --target x64Linux6gcc13.3.0CERT  .....

#end

#if ($use_mag)
Depending on the number of interfaces in the local machine, you might need
to limit what interfaces are actually used by RTI Connext Micro. You can find
that setting in file ${envMap.idlFileName}Qos.xml, XML element <allow_interfaces_list>.
#set ( $cmake_option_win_bat = "-DRTIME_MAG_FILES_eq_${envMap.idlFileName}.xml" )
#set ( $cmake_option_win = "-DRTIME_MAG_FILES=${envMap.idlFileName}.xml" )
#set ( $cmake_option = "-DRTIME_MAG_FILES=${envMap.idlFileName}.xml" )
#else
#set ( $cmake_option_win_bat = "" )
#set ( $cmake_option_win = "" )
#set ( $cmake_option = "" )
#end
#if ($use_secure)
#set ( $cmake_option_win_bat = "${cmake_option_win_bat} -DRTIME_SECURE_eq_true" )
#set ( $cmake_option_win = "${cmake_option_win} -DRTIME_SECURE=true" )
#set ( $cmake_option = "${cmake_option} -DRTIME_SECURE=true" )
#end
#if($use_cert_api_only)
#set ( $cmake_option_win_bat = "${cmake_option_win_bat} -DRTIME_CERT_eq_true" )
#set ( $cmake_option_win = "${cmake_option_win} -DRTIME_CERT=true" )
#set ( $cmake_option = "${cmake_option} -DRTIME_CERT=true" )
#end
#if ($use_psk)
#set ( $cmake_option_win_bat = "${cmake_option_win_bat} -DRTIME_LIGHTWEIGHT_SECURE_eq_true" )
#set ( $cmake_option_win = "${cmake_option_win} -DRTIME_LIGHTWEIGHT_SECURE=true" )
#set ( $cmake_option = "${cmake_option} -DRTIME_LIGHTWEIGHT_SECURE=true" )
#end

#if ($use_zcv2)
Zero Copy v2 requires that any IDL type file must start with @transfer_mode(SHMEM_REF).
#end
#if ($use_psk)
Set OPENSSLHOME to the install location of OpenSSL. This example has been tested with 3.5.5.
#end

The RTI Connext Micro installation includes a bash (Unix) and BAT (Windows)
script to simplify the invocation of CMake. These scripts are a convenient way
to invoke CMake with the correct options. E.g:

Linux
-----
cd "<${envMap.idlFileName}Application directory>"
rtime-make --config <Debug|Release> --build --name x64Linux3gcc4.8.2 --target Linux --source-dir . -G "Unix Makefiles" --delete ${cmake_option} [-DRTIME_IDL_ADD_REGENERATE_TYPESUPPORT_RULE=true]

Windows
-------
cd "<${envMap.idlFileName}Application directory>"
rtime-make.bat --config <Debug|Release> --build --name i86Win32VS2010 --target Windows --source-dir . -G "Visual Studio 10 2010" --delete ${cmake_option_win_bat} [-DRTIME_IDL_ADD_REGENERATE_TYPESUPPORT_RULE_eq_true]

Note: When building for Windows on any architecture other than x86, the user
must add the -A option for their architecture. For example, "-A x64".

Darwin
------
cd "<${envMap.idlFileName}Application directory>"
rtime-make --config <Debug|Release> --build --name x64Darwin17.3.0Clang9.0.0 --target Darwin --source-dir . -G "Unix Makefiles" --delete ${cmake_option} [-DRTIME_IDL_ADD_REGENERATE_TYPESUPPORT_RULE=true]

The executable can be found on directory "objs"


It is also possible to compile using CMake, if desired:


Linux
-----
cmake [-DRTIME_IDL_ADD_REGENERATE_TYPESUPPORT_RULE=true] [-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=<Debug|Release>] ${cmake_option} -G "Unix Makefiles" -B./<your build directory> -H. -DRTIME_TARGET_NAME=x64Linux3gcc4.8.2
cmake --build ./<your build directory> [--config <Debug|Release>]

Windows
-------
cmake [-DRTIME_IDL_ADD_REGENERATE_TYPESUPPORT_RULE=true] [-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=<Debug|Release>] ${cmake_option_win} -G "Visual Studio 10 2010" -B./<your build directory> -H. -DRTIME_TARGET_NAME=i86Win32VS2010
cmake --build ./<your build directory> [--config <Debug|Release>]

Note: When building for Windows on any architecture other than x86, the user
must add the -A option for their architecture. For example, "-A x64".

Darwin
------
cmake [-DRTIME_IDL_ADD_REGENERATE_TYPESUPPORT_RULE=true] [-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=<Debug|Release>] ${cmake_option} -G "Unix Makefiles" -B./<your build directory> -H. -DRTIME_TARGET_NAME=x64Darwin17.3.0Clang9.0.0
cmake --build ./<your build directory> [--config <Debug|Release>]

The executable can be found on ./objs

Option -DRTIME_IDL_ADD_REGENERATE_TYPESUPPORT_RULE=true adds a rule to regenerate
type support plugin source files if the input IDL/XML file changes.
Default value is 'false'.

Option -DEXCLUDE_SHMEM=true excludes the Shared memory libraries from being
included in builds.

Option -DEXCLUDE_ZCV2=true excludes the ZCv2 libraries from being
included in builds.

#if (${use_mag})
Option -DRTIME_MAG_FILES=<XML file with Application Generation definitions> adds
a rule to generate application support files from XML.
Default value is 'false'.

#end
#if ($use_secure)
Option -DRTIME_SECURE=true adds the secure libraries as a dependency for the linker
and invokes OpenSSL to generate all the secure certificates.
Default value is 'false'.

#end
#if($use_cert_api_only)
Option -DRTIME_CERT=true needs to be used to compile an example compatible with
cert API.
Default value is 'false'.

#end

------------------------------------------------------
Running ${envMap.idlFileName}_publisher and ${envMap.idlFileName}_subscriber
------------------------------------------------------

#if (!$use_mag)
By default the example uses two interfaces to receive samples. The names of these
interfaces are configured in file ${envMap.idlFileName}Application.${fileExtension}.
Some OS installations might use different names which would prevent communication.
For this reason it is recommended to use option -udp_intf <interface name>. Normally
you can use command 'ifconfig' (Linux) and 'ipconfig' (Windows) to know the name
of all available interfaces.
#else
By default the example uses all available interfaces to receive samples. This can
cause communication problems if the number of available interfaces is greater than
the default maximum in Connext Micro. For this reason it is recommended
restrict the number of interfaces to use by adding elements in 'allow_interfaces_list'
in file ${envMap.idlFileName}Qos.xml.
#end

E.g. in case the ${envMap.idlFileName} has been compiled for Linux i86Linux3gcc4.8.2 run the subscriber by typing:
#if (${use_mag})
#set ( $extra_options = "" )
#elseif (${use_secure} && ${use_udp})
#set ( $extra_options = " [-udp_intf <interface name>] [-peer <address>]" )
#elseif (${use_udp})
#set ( $extra_options = "[-domain <Domain_ID>] [-udp_intf <interface name>] [-peer <address>]" )
#else
#set ( $extra_options = "[-domain <Domain_ID>]" )
#end

objs/x64Linux3gcc4.8.2/${envMap.idlFileName}_subscriber ${extra_options} [-sleep <sleep_time>] [-count <count>]

and run the publisher by typing:

objs/x64Linux3gcc4.8.2/${envMap.idlFileName}_publisher ${extra_options} [-sleep <sleep_time>] [-count <count>]
#end  ##macro(readmeHowToCompileAndRun)

#macro(readmeSourceOverview)
Source Overview
===============

Type ${constructMap.nativeFQName} is provided by user and defined in file
"${envMap.idlFileName}.idl".

For convenience a CMakeList.txt is also generated so the example can be easily
compiled with CMake. A dependency can be added so the type-plugin interface
is regenerated if file ${envMap.idlFileName}.idl changes.

For the type to be usable by Connext Micro, type-support files must be
generated that implement a type-plugin interface. The CMakeList.txt file
will generate these support files, by invoking rtiddsgen. Note that rtiddsgen
can be invoked manually, with an example command like this:

#if ($use_cert_api_only)
"$(RTIMEHOME)/rtiddsmag/scripts/rtiddsgen" -micro -language $language -interpreted 0 ${envMap.idlFileName}.idl

Note: The -interpreted 0 option generates CDR type support instead of the
default XTypes support. This is required for the CERT profile, which does
not include the XTypes library.
#else
"$(RTIMEHOME)/rtiddsmag/scripts/rtiddsgen" -micro -language $language ${envMap.idlFileName}.idl
#end
#if ($use_mag)

For the applications to be usable by Connext Micro, application-support
files must be generated that define the necessary data structures with the
application's information. The CMakeList.txt file will generate these support
files, by invoking rtiddsmag. Note that rtiddsmag can be invoked manually,
with an example command like this:

"$(RTIMEHOME)/rtiddsmag/scripts/rtiddsmag" -language $language ${envMap.idlFileName}.xml -referencedFile ${envMap.idlFileName}Qos.xml
#end

#if ($use_mag)
The generated source files are ${envMap.idlFileName}.${fileExtension},
${envMap.idlFileName}Support.${fileExtension},
${envMap.idlFileName}Plugin.${fileExtension} and
${envMap.idlFileName}Appgen.${fileExtension}. Associated header files
are also generated.
The generated XML files are ${envMap.idlFileName}.xml
and ${envMap.idlFileName}Qos.xml.
#else
The generated source files are ${envMap.idlFileName}.${fileExtension},
${envMap.idlFileName}Support.${fileExtension}, and
${envMap.idlFileName}Plugin.${fileExtension}. Associated header files are also
generated.
#end

The DataWriter and DataReader of the type are managed in
${envMap.idlFileName}_publisher.${fileExtension} and
${envMap.idlFileName}_subscriber.${fileExtension}, respectively. The
DomainParticipant of each is managed in
${envMap.idlFileName}Application.${fileExtension}.
#end  ## #macro(readmeSourceOverview)

#macro(readmeExampleFilesOverview)
Example Files Overview
======================

${envMap.idlFileName}Application.${fileExtension}:
This file contains the logic for creating an application. This includes steps
for configuring discovery and creating a DomainParticipant. This file also
includes code for registering a type with the DomainParticipant.

${envMap.idlFileName}_publisher.${fileExtension}:
This file contains the logic for creating a Publisher and a DataWriter, and
sending data.

${envMap.idlFileName}_subscriber.${fileExtension}:
This file contains the logic for creating a Subscriber and a DataReader, a
DataReaderListener, and listening for data.

${envMap.idlFileName}Plugin.${fileExtension}:
This file creates the plugin for the ${envMap.idlFileName} data type.  This
file contains the code for serializing and deserializing the ${envMap.idlFileName}
type, creating, copying, printing and deleting the ${envMap.idlFileName} type,
determining the size of the serialized type, and handling hashing a key, and
creating the plug-in.

${envMap.idlFileName}Support.${fileExtension}:
This file defines the ${envMap.idlFileName} type and its typed DataWriter,
DataReader, and Sequence.

${envMap.idlFileName}.${fileExtension}:
This file contains the APIs for managing the ${envMap.idlFileName} type.
#if ($use_mag)

${envMap.idlFileName}Appgen.${fileExtension}:
This file contains the necessary data structures with the application's
information.

${envMap.idlFileName}.xml:
This file contains the Publisher and Subscriber applications definition. That
is the types, topics, DomainParticipants, DataWriter and DataReaders that each
application uses.

${envMap.idlFileName}Qos.xml:
This file contains the QoS used by the DDS entities created in the Publisher and
Subscriber applications.
#end
#end ## #macro(readmeExampleFilesOverview)

